    "kind": "place_history",
    "content": "# Place Notes — Grayharbor and the Undervault\n\n## Grayharbor\n\nGrayharbor is a port city of six terraces cut into black basalt cliffs above\na deep-water harbor. The terraces are linked by stair-streets and rope\nlifts; the lowest terrace floods at spring tide and everyone pretends this\nis fine. Principal institutions: the Terrace Council (civic government),\nthe Grand Weighing House on the third terrace (all cargo and salvage is\nweighed, taxed, and brokered there), the Cinder Compact hall, and the\nSurveyors' Guild. The fish market and Netmaker's Row occupy the second\nterrace. Lamplighters maintain the terrace lamps on a strict dusk circuit;\nthe lamp-house is their guild hall.\n\nNotable residents referenced in the manuscript: Odessa Vane-Kir, the\nWeighing House's senior salvage broker, called Dess by those she permits;\nher office sits behind an unmarked door at the back of the Weighing House,\nand her family has brokered Vault salvage since before the Compact existed.\n\n## The Undervault\n\nThe Undervault — delvers simply say \"the Vault\" — is a sealed pre-Collapse\ncomplex beneath the harbor cliffs, entered through the Salt Door: a carved\nstone slab at the cliff base, submerged except at low tide. The spring\nquake cracked the Door's seal after eleven centuries; the Cinder Compact\nclaimed delving rights within the week.\n\nKnown interior, per Compact surveys: an antechamber with twelve pillars, a\nfloor channel, a dais bearing a dormant warding circle, and an inner gate\nof dark metal locked by an emberglass socket. Below the gate, a gallery\npatrolled by husks — the Vault's dead-that-tally, animate remains in\ncorroded old-world armor — and beyond it, an underground city whose\navenues all channel toward a central well ringed by twelve seated metal\nfigures.\n\nHazards: warding circles (dormant, violently dischargeable); husks\n(dangerous in close quarters, vulnerable to sorrowsteel and aether\ndischarge); and Vaultburn — a creeping gray miasma that infects the living,\nsuppresses aether recovery, and marks the skin with pewter threads. Wardlight\nlanterns burning aether-oil keep the miasma at bay. Established treatment:\nsalt, cedar oil, and whitethorn ash over roughly a week, a Vane-Kir family\nremedy predating the Compact.\n\nThe Emberglass Key: a palm-sized faceted shard of orange glass, warm to the\ntouch, recovered from the antechamber channel after the ward discharge. It\nis the only known means of opening the inner gate."
